# Dates are always YYYY-MM-DD

Whatever the browser's or computer's locale, a date reads `2026-11-23`; a timestamp adds the
time as `2026-11-23 14:05`. Never `23.11.2026`, `11/23/2026`, `23 Nov` or `November 23`.

## Showing dates

Use the helpers in `src/lib/formatDate.ts`; never the locale APIs.

```tsx
// ❌ BAD: follows the computer's regional format
new Date(value).toLocaleDateString();
new Intl.DateTimeFormat(undefined, { day: "numeric", month: "short" }).format(d);
format(d, "d MMM"); // date-fns

// ✅ GOOD
formatDate(value);      // 2026-11-23
formatDateTime(value);  // 2026-11-23 14:05
new Date(utcMs).toISOString().slice(0, 10); // a UTC timestamp's calendar day
```

## Typing dates

Never `<input type="date">` (or `datetime-local`): it shows the regional format. Use
`DesignDateInput` from `src/components/ds/DesignDateInput.tsx` — value and `onChange` are
`YYYY-MM-DD` strings, and it reads other typed formats into `YYYY-MM-DD`.

## Storing and importing

- Dataset and period values are saved as `YYYY-MM-DD`. Convert imported values with
  `toIsoDate` / `normalizeDateColumn` from `server/shared/dates.ts`; when month-first vs
  day-first can't be told, ask the person instead of guessing.
- Excel dates go through `cellText` in `src/lib/spreadsheetFile.ts`.
- A bare `YYYY-MM-DD` is a calendar day: don't pass it through `new Date()` and back via UTC
  (it can shift a day); compare and sort the strings directly.

## Copy

Dates written in UI text, toasts, AI summaries, docs and placeholders use `YYYY-MM-DD` too
(placeholder: `"YYYY-MM-DD"`). Quarters keep their own label (`2026-Q4`).
